  "OverallSummary": "This case involves G.research, LLC's failure to comply with Form CRS disclosure requirements from June 30, 2020, to May 1, 2023. The firm failed to disclose its own and its control affiliates' disciplinary history in its Form CRS, answering 'No' to the question about legal or disciplinary history when both the firm and four of its control affiliates had reportable disciplinary history. This constituted a willful violation of Section 17(a)(1) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and Exchange Act Rule 17a-14. Additionally, from June 30, 2020, to the present, G.research failed to establish and maintain a reasonable supervisory system, including written supervisory procedures, to ensure compliance with Form CRS requirements, violating FINRA Rules 3110 and 2010. The firm's WSPs initially contained no provisions for Form CRS compliance, and even after updates in July 2021, they failed to identify responsible individuals or establish procedures for reviewing and updating the Form CRS. As a result, G.research was censured, fined $30,000, and required to submit a certification confirming implementation of reasonably designed supervisory procedures for Form CRS compliance. No specific individual customers were identified in the case, but all retail customers of the firm were affected by receiving incomplete disclosure information. This case does not involve Care Obligation violations under Regulation Best Interest, but rather focuses on disclosure and supervision failures."
